# How much of the system each stack claims to hold. One row per filing.
#
# A filing is never the system
#   There is one system; a filing holds the layers of it that mattered to whoever filed. Reading a
#   stack as an enumeration of the system is what turns a second document's extra layer into an
#   accusation that the first omitted something, when it is two projections of one thing.
#
# The third extent is the one two values would crush
#   `scoped` says somebody established what lies outside and excluded it on purpose. `unbounded`
#   says nobody looked. Rendered identically, a bounded selection is indistinguishable from an
#   unexamined one: the same distinction the coupling search draws one level down.
#
# `complete` is falsifiable in principle only
#   It is falsifiable in principle and by nothing in this corpus. What would refute it is another
#   filing holding a layer of the same system that this one lacks, and no document names a
#   system, only a filing. So it belongs in the owed list rather than in a query that looks as
#   though it checks something.
-- pm:Stack/pm:scope, with its pm:basis.
SELECT ss.filing, ss.extent, ss.basis, ss.absent
FROM pm.stack_scope ss
